ts \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(2\\neq1\\)) and not on \\(\\ell_{2}\\) (its \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(2\\neq1\\)).  \nHence a third line must pass through \\((2,2)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3. A sunny line through \\((2,2)\\)\n\nTake the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{3}:\\;y=x.\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\); consequently \\(\\ell_{3}\\) is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (slope \\(0\\)), not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical), and not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). Hence \\(\\ell_{3}\\) is sunny.\n\n\\(\\ell_{3}\\) passes through \\((2,2)\\) and also through \\((1,1)\\), a point already covered by \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Verification\n\nThe three lines are distinct:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is vertical,\n* \\(\\ell_{2}\\) is horizontal,\n* \\(\\ell_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\).\n\nExactly one of them (\\(\\ell_{3}\\)) is sunny.\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line covering it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & \\ell_{1},\\;\\ell_{2},\\;\\ell_{3}\\\\\n(1,2) & \\ell_{1}\\\\\n(1,3) & \\ell_{1}\\\\\n(2,1) & \\ell_{2}\\\\\n(3,1) & \\ell_{2}\\\\\n(2,2) & \\ell_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus all conditions of the problem are satisfied with \\(n=3\\) lines and exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line. \u220e"
