------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The non\u2011sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S\\)\n\nSuppose \\(N\\) contains three points of \\(S\\).  Because the only directions\nallowed for \\(N\\) are those in (1), the three points must be collinear either\nvertically, horizontally, or along a line of slope \\(-1\\).  In each case the\nremaining three points of \\(S\\) are pairwise joined by a vector of one of the\nforbidden directions:\n\n* If \\(N\\) is vertical, say \\(x=1\\), then the remaining points are\n  \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\); the pairs \\((2,1)-(2,2)\\) (vertical) and\n  \\((2,1)-(3,1)\\) (horizontal) are forbidden, and \\((2,2)-(3,1)\\) has slope\n  \\(-1\\).\n\n* If \\(N\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=1\\), the remaining points are\n  \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\); the pairs \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\) (vertical) and\n  \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\) (horizontal) are forbidden, and \\((1,3)-(2,2)\\) has slope\n  \\(-1\\).\n\n* If \\(N\\) has slope \\(-1\\), say \\(x+y=4\\), the remaining points are\n  \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); the pairs \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\) (vertical) and\n  \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\) (horizontal) are forbidden, and \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\) has slope\n  \\(-1\\).\n\nThus the four points that would have to be covered by the two sunny lines\ncontain a pair whose connecting line has a forbidden direction, which is\nimpossible because a sunny line cannot contain such a pair.  Hence **\\(N\\) can\ncontain at most two points of \\(S\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The non\u2011sunny line must contain exactly two points of \\(S\\)\n\nIf \\(N\\) contained only one point of \\(S\\), the other five points would have to\nbe covered by two sunny lines, each of which can contain at most two points of\n\\(S\\) (any three points of \\(S\\) are never collinear with an allowed slope).\nTwo lines can therefore cover at most four points, contradicting the need to\ncover five.  Consequently **\\(N\\) contains exactly two points of \\(S\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustion of the possible non\u2011sunny lines\n\nAll lines parallel to the directions in (1) that pass through at least two\npoints of \\(S\\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3,\\\\\n&y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3,\\\\\n&x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach of these nine lines indeed contains exactly two points of \\(S\\).  \nFor each line in (2) we list the four points of \\(S\\) that remain uncovered\nand show that they cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n| \\(N\\) | points on \\(N\\) | remaining points \\(R\\) | forbidden pairs in \\(R\\) |\n|------|----------------|------------------------|--------------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2)\\) | \\((1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((2,1)-(2,2)\\) vertical, \\((2,1)-(3,1)\\) horizontal, \\((2,2)-(3,1)\\) slope \\(-1\\) |\n| \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\) vertical, \\((1,1)-(3,1)\\) horizontal, \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (allowed) but then \\((1,1)-(1,3)\\) vertical remains uncovered |\n| \\(x=3\\) | \\((3,1)\\) \u2013 only one point \u2192 impossible (see \u00a71) |\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\) vertical, \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\) horizontal, \\((1,3)-(2,2)\\) slope \\(-1\\) |\n| \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\) horizontal, \\((1,1)-(1,3)\\) vertical, \\((2,1)-(3,1)\\) horizontal |\n| \\(y=3\\) | \\((1,3)\\) \u2013 only one point \u2192 impossible |\n| \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) \u2013 only one point \u2192 impossible |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\) slope \\(1\\) (allowed) but then \\((1,3)-(3,1)\\) slope \\(-1\\) remains uncovered |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) \u2013 three points \u2192 contradicts \u00a71 |\n\nIn every admissible case the set \\(R\\) of four uncovered points contains at\nleast one pair whose connecting line has a forbidden direction (vertical,\nhorizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).  A sunny line cannot contain such a pair, so two\nsunny lines cannot cover all of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll possibilities for the non\u2011sunny line lead to a contradiction.  Hence no\ncollection of three distinct lines can cover the six required points with\nexactly two of the lines sunny.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
