y** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following families of three distinct lines realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  \\(\\{x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\}\\) (all non\u2011sunny).\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  \\(\\{y=1,\\;x+y=3,\\;y=2x-1\\}\\).  \n  The line \\(y=2x-1\\) has slope \\(2\\) (sunny) and passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\); the other two points are on the two diagonal lines.\n\n* \\(k=2\\):  \\(\\{y=1,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=3x-2\\}\\).  \n  The two slanted lines have slopes \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) (both sunny) and together with the horizontal line cover all six points.\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  \\(\\{y=2x-1,\\;y=3x-2,\\;y=4x-3\\}\\).  \n  All three lines have slopes \\(2,3,4\\) (sunny) and each contains two points of \\(S_3\\); together they cover the whole set.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive step\n\nAssume that for a fixed \\(n\\ge3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{n,k}=\\{L^{(n,k)}_1,\\dots ,L^{(n,k)}_{\\,n}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny,  \n* \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L^{(n,k)}_i\\supseteq S_n\\).\n\nWe construct families for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Keeping the number of sunny lines\n\nAdd the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\setminus S_n\\) lie on \\(D_{n+1}\\); the points of \\(S_n\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{\\,n+1,k}:=\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\((n+1)\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly the same \\(k\\) sunny lines (because \\(D_{n+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one\n\nReplace the newly added diagonal line \\(D_{n+1}\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}:\\; y = -\\frac{n-1}{n}\\,x+\\frac{n+2}{n},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the two extreme points \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,2)\\) of the new diagonal.  \nIts slope \\(-\\dfrac{n-1}{n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(S_{n+1}\\) is sunny.\n\nAll interior points of the new diagonal have the form \\((a,n+2-a)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\).  \nEach such point already belongs to the old diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and hence is covered by the original configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\).  \nThus after the replacement every point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) remains covered, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{\\,n+1,k+1}:=\\bigl(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\setminus\\{D_{n+1}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Completion of the induction\n\nStarting from the families \\(\\mathcal C_{3,k}\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le3\\)) given in the base case, we may apply repeatedly the operation of \u00a72.1 (which leaves \\(k\\) unchanged) and the operation of \u00a72.2 (which raises \\(k\\) by one).  \nConsequently, for every integer \\(m\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal C_{m,k}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Explicit constructions for the extreme values\n\nFor completeness we give direct constructions that do not rely on induction.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) antidiagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n  \\]\n\n