"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff its slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n   All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n2. **Extremal constructions.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) antidiagonal lines  \n   \\[\n   L_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n   are all non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) put  \n   \\[\n   M_i:\\;y=i\\,x-(i-1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(M_i\\) equals \\(i\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); hence each \\(M_i\\) is sunny and the lines are distinct.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a>1\\) we have\n   \\[\n   b=i a-(i-1)\\iff i=\\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the fraction \\(\\dfrac{b-1}{a-1}\\) is a positive integer not larger than \\(n\\); therefore (2) determines a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_i\\).  \n   When \\(a=1\\) the only possible point is \\((1,1)\\), which belongs to \\(M_1\\).  Thus the family \\(\\{M_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers all of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Inductive step.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) there exists a set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{m,k}=\\{L^{(m,k)}_1,\\dots ,L^{(m,k)}_{\\,m}\\}\n   \\]\n   of \\(m\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n\n   * exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny,  \n   * \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{j=1}^{m}L^{(m,k)}_j\\supseteq S_m\\).\n\n   From \\(\\mathcal C_{m,k}\\) we construct families for \\(m+1\\).\n\n   * **(i) Keeping the number of sunny lines.**  \n     Add the antidiagonal line  \n     \\[\n     D_{m+1}:\\;x+y=m+2 .\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n     The points of \\(S_{m+1}\\setminus S_m\\) are precisely the points of the new antidiagonal (3); all points of \\(S_m\\) are already covered.  \n     Hence\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal C_{\\,m+1,k}:=\\mathcal C_{m,k}\\cup\\{D_{m+1}\\}\n     \\]\n     is a family of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{m+1}\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (because \\(D_{m+1}\\) has slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n   * **(ii) Increasing the number of sunny lines by one.**  \n     In \\(\\mathcal C_{m,k}\\) choose a line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); such a line exists unless \\(k=m\\), in which case we already have the maximal number of sunny lines and need not increase \\(k\\).  \n     Remove the antidiagonal line \\(D_{m+1}\\) added in (i) and replace it by the sunny line\n     \\[\n     S_{m+1}:\\;y=-\\frac{m-1}{m}\\,x+\\frac{m+2}{m},\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n     which passes through the two extreme points \\((1,m+1)\\) and \\((m+1,2)\\) of the new antidiagonal.  \n     The slope \\(-\\dfrac{m-1}{m}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(S_{m+1}\\) is sunny.  \n\n     Every interior point of the new antidiagonal has the form \\((a,m+2-a)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le m\\).  Such a point already belongs to the old antidiagonal \\(x+y=m+1\\) and consequently is covered by \\(\\mathcal C_{m,k}\\).  Hence after the replacement all points of \\(S_{m+1}\\) remain covered, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one:\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal C_{\\,m+1,k+1}:=\n     \\bigl(\\mathcal C_{m,k}\\cup\\{D_{m+1}\\}\\bigr)\\setminus\\{D_{m+1}\\}\\cup\\{S_{m+1}\\}\n     =\\mathcal C_{m,k}\\cup\\{S_{m+1}\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   By repeatedly applying (i) (which leaves \\(k\\) unchanged) and (ii) (which raises \\(k\\) by one) we obtain, for the fixed integer \\(m+1\\), a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{\\,m+1,k}\\) for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m+1\\).\n\n4. **Completion of the induction.**  \n   The explicit families for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) given in step\u202f2 provide the required bases for the induction (the case \\(n=3\\) is a concrete illustration).  The inductive construction of step\u202f3 shows that, assuming the statement true for some \\(m\\), it is also true for \\(m+1\\).  Therefore, by induction on \\(n\\), for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   By definition a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines and at least \\(0\\) sunny lines, so no other integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion** \u2013 The admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely all integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
