            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *For \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) let*  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c\n   \\]\n\n   *be the anti‑diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\).  Such a line is **not** sunny.  \n   The set of required points is*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Extremal construction (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   The family \\(\\{D_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because each \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Upper bound.**  \n   Obviously at most \\(n\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, so any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n4. **General construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n\n   *Step 4.1 – keep the largest anti‑diagonals.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{D_c\\mid c=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(S_k\\) contains \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   All points belonging to the diagonals \\(c=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\) are already covered.\n\n   *Step 4.2 – the uncovered part.*  \n   The remaining points are those on the small diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal Q_k=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{k+1} D_c\\cap\\mathcal P_n .\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the diagonal \\(D_c\\) contributes exactly \\(c-1\\) points, so  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal Q_k|=\\sum_{c=2}^{k+1}(c-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly \\(k\\) such diagonals; denote them by \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_k\\) where\n   \\(C_i=D_{i+1}\\) (so \\(|C_i|=i\\)).\n\n   *Step 4.3 – a matching of points to sunny lines.*  \n   We shall construct \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i\\cap C_j\\neq\\varnothing\\quad\\text{for every }1\\le i,j\\le k,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   and each intersection point is distinct.  \n   Condition (1) guarantees that the \\(k\\) lines together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) (because \\(|\\mathcal Q_k|=k(k+1)/2\\) and each line meets each diagonal in exactly one point, giving a total of \\(k\\cdot k\\) incidences; the \\(k\\) “extra’’ incidences are the points \\((1,1)\\) that belong to every diagonal and are counted only once).\n\n   To obtain such lines we use Hall’s marriage theorem.  \n   Consider the bipartite graph whose left part consists of the \\(k\\) diagonals\n   \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_k\\) and whose right part consists of \\(k\\) abstract vertices\n   \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_k\\) (these will become the lines).  \n   Connect \\(C_i\\) to \\(R_j\\) by an edge for every lattice point of \\(C_i\\); we shall later choose exactly one edge incident with each \\(R_j\\).\n\n   For any subset \\(X\\subseteq\\{C_1,\\dots ,C_k\\}\\) let \\(|X|=t\\).  \n   The total number of points lying on the diagonals of \\(X\\) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{C_i\\in X}|C_i|=\\sum_{i\\in I} i = \\frac{t(t+1)}2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(I\\) is the set of indices of the chosen diagonals.  \n   Since each right‑hand vertex \\(R_j\\) can be incident with at most one point from a given diagonal, the neighbourhood of \\(X\\) in the graph contains at least \\(\\frac{t(t+1)}2\\) distinct right‑hand vertices.  \n   Because we have only \\(k\\) right‑hand vertices and \\(t\\le k\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{t(t+1)}2\\ge t\\qquad\\text{for all }t\\ge1,\n   \\]\n\n   so the Hall condition \\(|N(X)|\\ge |X|\\) holds for every \\(X\\).  \n   Consequently a system of distinct representatives exists: we can select\n   one lattice point from each diagonal \\(C_i\\) and assign it to a distinct line \\(L_j\\).\n\n   *Step 4.4 – defining the sunny lines.*  \n   Choose the representatives as follows: for each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{j}=(j,1)\\in C_j .\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed \\((j,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=j+1\\).)  \n   For each \\(j\\) pick also the point  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_{j}=(1,j)\\in C_j .\n   \\]\n\n   The two points \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) are distinct, and the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:=\\overline{P_jQ_j}\n   \\]\n\n   has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{j-1}{1-j}= -1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is **not** allowed (it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   To obtain a sunny line we modify the second point slightly: for each \\(j\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   Q'_j=(1,j+1)\\in C_{j+1}\\quad (j<k),\\qquad \n   Q'_k=(1,k)\\in C_k .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_j\\) through \\(P_j=(j,1)\\) and \\(Q'_j\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(j+1)-1}{1-j}= \\frac{j}{1-j},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n   Moreover, for each diagonal \\(C_i\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\), and the point\n   \\((1,i+1)\\) (or \\((1,i)\\) when \\(i=k\\)) lies on \\(L_{i-1}\\).  Consequently each diagonal\n   \\(C_i\\) meets **eve