resentatives exists: we can select\n   one lattice point from each diagonal \\(C_i\\) and assign it to a distinct line \\(L_j\\).\n\n   *Step 4.4 – defining the sunny lines.*  \n   Choose the representatives as follows: for each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{j}=(j,1)\\in C_j .\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed \\((j,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=j+1\\).)  \n   For each \\(j\\) pick also the point  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_{j}=(1,j)\\in C_j .\n   \\]\n\n   The two points \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) are distinct, and the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:=\\overline{P_jQ_j}\n   \\]\n\n   has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{j-1}{1-j}= -1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is **not** allowed (it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   To obtain a sunny line we modify the second point slightly: for each \\(j\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   Q'_j=(1,j+1)\\in C_{j+1}\\quad (j<k),\\qquad \n   Q'_k=(1,k)\\in C_k .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_j\\) through \\(P_j=(j,1)\\) and \\(Q'_j\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(j+1)-1}{1-j}= \\frac{j}{1-j},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n   Moreover, for each diagonal \\(C_i\\) the point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\), and the point\n   \\((1,i+1)\\) (or \\((1,i)\\) when \\(i=k\\)) lies on \\(L_{i-1}\\).  Consequently each diagonal\n   \\(C_i\\) meets **every** line \\(L_j\\) in exactly one lattice point, establishing (1).\n\n   *Step 4.5 – the final family.*  \n   The required set of \\(n\\) lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k = S_k\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   It contains \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny anti‑diagonal lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   By construction the \\(n-k\\) anti‑diagonals cover all points of the large\n   diagonals, while the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover all points of the small\n   diagonals \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).  Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at\n   least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration with\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   As observed in step 3, a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more\n   than \\(n\\) sunny lines, and obviously cannot contain a negative number of\n   sunny lines.  Hence no \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is possible.\n\nCombining the existence part (step 4) with the trivial upper bound (step 3) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
