\\{D_2,D_3,D_4\\}\\);\n   * \\(k=1\\): \\(\\{y=x\\}\\cup\\{D_3,D_4\\}\\);\n   * \\(k=2\\): \\(\\{y=x,\\;y=x-1\\}\\cup\\{D_4\\}\\);\n   * \\(k=3\\):  \n     \\[\n     L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n     L_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n     L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{13}{2}.\n     \\]\n     The three sunny lines together contain all six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).\n\n   Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n4. **Inductive step.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   From \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) we construct families for \\(n+1\\).\n\n   *4.1  Keeping the same number of sunny lines (\\(k\\le n\\)).*  \n   Add the non‑sunny anti‑diagonal line  \n   \\[\n   D_{\\,n+2}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n   \\]\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) lies on this new line, while all points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \n   The resulting family\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{D_{\\,n+2}\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n+1}\\).\n\n   *4.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one (\\(k=n+1\\)).*  \n   Start from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (which exists by the induction hypothesis).  \n   Add the sunny line\n   \\[\n   S_{n+1}:\\; \\text{the line through }(n+1,1)\\text{ and }(1,n+2).\n   \\]\n   Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m_{n+1}= \\frac{(n+2)-1}{1-(n+1)} = -\\frac{n+1}{n},\n   \\]\n   which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(S_{n+1}\\) is sunny.  \n   The new point \\((n+1,1)\\) (the only point of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n+1}\\) that was not present in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)) lies on \\(S_{n+1}\\); all other points of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n+1}\\) already belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\